%I A397659 #24 Aug 02 2026 10:54:41
%S A397659 1,72,6210,574200,55080000,5404848768,538599582576,54273600469440,
%T A397659 5515363806653250,564193601485470000,58021541733797753364,
%U A397659 5993026527708366095088,621281801391988176268200,64606565034122652022176000
%N A397659 a(n) = 108^n * [z^n] hypergeom([1/6, 1/3], [1], z)^3/sqrt(1-z).
%C A397659 Conjugate Eisenstein-branch sequence at the mixed CM point (1/6, 1/3; 1).
%C A397659 Let u = eta(3*tau)^12/eta(tau)^12, t = u/(1+27*u)^2, H_mix = q/t, and C_0 = 3*E_{5,chi_0,chi_3}. Then a(n) = [q^n] (1+27*u(q))*C_0(q)*H_mix(q)^n.
%C A397659 In the notation of Shvets, arXiv:2605.19773, where C^(eps) := C_0 - 27*eps*u*C_0 and A_m^(eps) := [q^m] C^(eps)(q)*H_mix(q)^m, this is the branch eps = -1, i.e. a(m) = A_m^(-1). The branch eps = +1 is A393983.
%C A397659 The hypergeometric form in the Name follows from the Lagrange-Burmann theorem of that paper together with the identity 1 - 108*t = (1-27*u)^2/(1+27*u)^2, which gives C^(-1) = C_mix/sqrt(1-108*t), where C_mix = (1-27*u)*C_0 is the mixed Fricke-trace direction realizing A393983.
%C A397659 Theorem 5.4 of Shvets (op. cit.) proves the first-digit Cartier parity law A393983(m*p^r) == A_m^(chi_3(p)^r) (mod p) for every prime p >= 5 and all m, r >= 0. Specializing to inert primes, i.e. p >= 5 with p == 2 (mod 3), gives A393983(m*p^(2*r+1)) == a(m) (mod p) for all m, r >= 0, and A393983(m*p^(2*r)) == A393983(m) (mod p).
%C A397659 In particular a(1) = 72 gives Corollary 5.5 of that paper: A393983(p) == 72 (mod p) at every inert prime p >= 5. Since A393983(1) = 18, the split-style congruence A393983(m*p) == A393983(m) fails already modulo p at inert primes.
%H A397659 Alex Shvets, <a href="/A397659/b397659.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..500</a>
%H A397659 Frits Beukers, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.03301">Supercongruences using modular forms</a>, arXiv:2403.03301 [math.NT], 2024-2025.
%H A397659 L. Long, F.-T. Tu, N. Yui, and W. Zudilin, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2021.108058">Supercongruences for rigid hypergeometric Calabi-Yau threefolds</a>, Adv. Math. 393 (2021), Paper No. 108058.
%H A397659 Alex Shvets, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.19773">Split-prime supercongruence at the mixed CM point (1/6, 1/3; 1)</a>, arXiv:2605.19773 [math.NT], 2026.
%F A397659 G.f.: hypergeom([1/6, 1/3], [1], 108*x)^3/sqrt(1-108*x).
%F A397659 Equivalently, by the Euler transformation, g.f. = (1-108*x)*hypergeom([5/6, 2/3], [1], 108*x)^3.
%F A397659 The g.f. equals the g.f. of A393983 divided by sqrt(1-108*x); hence a(n) = Sum_{k=0..n} binomial(2*k,k)*27^k*A393983(n-k).
%F A397659 a(n) satisfies the order-2 recurrence (n+2)^4*a(n+2) - 6*(36*n^4 + 234*n^3 + 586*n^2 + 669*n + 293)*a(n+1) + 324*(n+1)*(2*n+3)*(3*n+4)*(6*n+7)*a(n) = 0, with a(0) = 1, a(1) = 72.
%F A397659 With C_0, u and H_mix as in Comments, a(n) = [q^n] (1+27*u(q))*C_0(q)*H_mix(q)^n.
%F A397659 a(n) ~ Gamma(1/3)^9 * 2^(2*n - 7) * 3^(3*n + 3) / (Pi^(13/2) * n^(1/2)).
%e A397659 a(2) = A393983(2) + binomial(2,1)*27*A393983(1) + binomial(4,2)*27^2*A393983(0) = 864 + 2*27*18 + 6*729*1 = 864 + 972 + 4374 = 6210.
%p A397659 A397659 := proc(n) option remember;
%p A397659   if n = 0 then 1
%p A397659   elif n = 1 then 72
%p A397659   else (6*(36*n^4-54*n^3+46*n^2-19*n+3)*A397659(n-1) - 324*(n-1)*(2*n-1)*(3*n-2)*(6*n-5)*A397659(n-2))/n^4
%p A397659   fi
%p A397659 end proc:
%p A397659 seq(A397659(n), n=0..20);
%t A397659 CoefficientList[Normal[Series[Hypergeometric2F1[1/6, 1/3, 1, 108*x]^3/Sqrt[1 - 108*x], {x, 0, 20}]], x]
%o A397659 (PARI) a(n) = {my(A=vector(max(n+1,2))); A[1]=1; A[2]=72; for(k=0, n-2, A[k+3] = (6*(36*k^4+234*k^3+586*k^2+669*k+293)*A[k+2] - 324*(k+1)*(2*k+3)*(3*k+4)*(6*k+7)*A[k+1])/(k+2)^4); A[n+1]}
%o A397659 (PARI) Vec(hypergeom([1/6, 1/3], [1], 108*z)^3/sqrt(1 - 108*z))
%Y A397659 Cf. A393983 (the branch eps = +1 at the same CM point), A393810, A394679, A394680, A397472.
%K A397659 nonn,easy
%O A397659 0,2
